//! Headset options, built once and handed to `Config.xr` or `Settings.xr`,
//! and the controllers `Effect.haptic` vibrates (`Xr.Left`, `Xr.Right`).
//!
//! Each knob belongs to one bucket, by when the shell can honour it:
//! `refreshRate`, `passthrough`, `scene`, `floorLocked` and `msaa` are read
//! once at load, so they go in `config`; `foveation`, `passthroughEnabled`,
//! `streamDepth` and `streamCamera` apply live, so they go in `settings`. A knob in the wrong bucket is a teaching
//! error naming the right one. Shells without a headset ignore these options
//! (except where a knob says otherwise); desktop still reports them in
//! `GET /state`.
//!
//! ```functor
//! let config = Config.create() |> Config.xr(Xr.create() |> Xr.refreshRate(90))
//! let settings = (model) =>
//!   Settings.create() |> Settings.xr(Xr.create() |> Xr.foveation(Xr.High))
//! ```

/// An opaque set of headset options.
type t = host

/// A fixed foveated rendering level: how coarsely the periphery of each eye
/// image is shaded, from `Off` (full resolution everywhere) to `High`
/// (coarsest periphery, most GPU saved).
type level =
  | Off
  | Low
  | Medium
  | High

/// Where `streamDepth` or `streamCamera` sends frames, and how many a second.
type streamOptions = { url: string, hz: float }

/// A controller, for `Effect.haptic`.
type hand =
  | Left
  | Right

/// No options set: every knob keeps the shell's default.
let create : () => t
/// Request a display refresh rate in Hz; the options are last for piping.
///
/// A `config` knob. The rate must be positive. The headset honours it only if
/// its display offers that rate (Quest 3: 72, 80, 90, and 120 once enabled in
/// the headset's settings); otherwise it logs a warning and keeps its default.
/// `functor run vr --refresh-rate` overrides it.
let refreshRate : (float, t) => t
/// Request mixed reality: the headset's camera feed of the room, shown behind
/// frames drawn with `Frame.withPassthrough`. The options are last for piping.
///
/// A `config` knob. On Quest it prepares `XR_FB_passthrough` for the run and
/// starts the feed; a headset without it logs a warning (also appended to the
/// `functor run vr` load response) and frames show a stand-in room instead
/// (see `Frame.withPassthrough`). Desktop and web always show the stand-in.
/// In passthrough,
/// 1 unit = 1 metre in the headset's floor-level stage space, so a model
/// authored in metres appears at true scale.
let passthrough : (bool, t) => t
/// Request scene understanding: the headset senses the depth of the real room
/// every frame, for passes that shade onto its surfaces. The options are last
/// for piping.
///
/// A `config` knob. On Quest 3 and 3S it starts `XR_META_environment_depth`
/// for the run, and `GET /state` reports it as `xr.depth`. A headset without
/// it logs a warning, also appended to the `functor run vr` load response.
/// Wherever no camera feed is composited — desktop, web, or a paused feed —
/// the frame's stand-in room (`Frame.withStandInRoom`) supplies the depth
/// instead.
let scene : (bool, t) => t
/// Anchor the headset's tracking to the floor under you, for an opaque game
/// that needs your real height. The options are last for piping.
///
/// A `config` knob. Without it the rig centres on your head's full pose when
/// tracking starts: `xr.head` starts at the origin, and "up" keeps any tilt
/// your head had then. With it the rig centres on the floor under your head,
/// level and facing your heading:
/// - `Frame.camera`'s eye stands for that floor point, so keep the camera level
///   and author it at your player's feet. The headset draws your eye at
///   `Frame.camera`'s eye plus `xr.head`.
/// - Rig-local `y = 0` is the floor, so `xr.head`'s `y` is your eye height
///   above it.
/// - A recentre (the headset's own, or a new tracking space) moves only your
///   heading and floor position; heights survive it.
///
/// On Quest the floor comes from the stage (`STAGE`) space. Without one the
/// floor is assumed 1.6 m below the head where tracking starts. Desktop
/// `--emulate-xr` stands the emulated head at `--xr-eye-height` (default
/// 1.6 m) and draws from it. With no head to compose (plain desktop, web)
/// the view is `Frame.camera` itself, so add an eye height when
/// `Input.snapshot`'s `xr` is `None`.
let floorLocked : (bool, t) => t
/// Multisample the eye images: `samples` (1, 2 or 4) coverage samples per
/// pixel smooth thin, high-contrast edges that crawl at a distance. The
/// options are last for piping.
///
/// A `config` knob, applied each time the project loads. The default is 1 (no
/// multisampling). MSAA costs GPU time, so measure before shipping it. On
/// Quest the samples stay in tile memory and resolve as each eye finishes
/// (`GL_EXT_multisampled_render_to_texture`); a runtime without that renders
/// with 1, and `GET /state`'s `views[].samples` reports the count in use. It
/// smooths the eye pass only, not `Frame.withRenderTarget` passes. Desktop and
/// web ignore it.
let msaa : (float, t) => t
/// Run (`true`) or pause (`false`) the camera feed `Xr.passthrough` prepared;
/// the options are last for piping.
///
/// A `settings` knob, applied whenever its value changes. Without it the feed
/// runs for as long as `config` requests passthrough. While paused,
/// `Frame.withPassthrough` frames show the stand-in room.
let passthroughEnabled : (bool, t) => t
/// Pin the foveation level; the options are last for piping.
///
/// A `settings` knob, applied whenever its value changes. Without it the
/// headset uses its default: `High`, lowered dynamically while the GPU has
/// headroom. An explicit level is fixed — the runtime does not scale it.
let foveation : (level, t) => t
/// POST the environment depth to `url`, `hz` frames a second, one
/// `functor.depth-frame/1` per request (see `docs/vr.md`, "Depth
/// streaming"); the options are last for piping.
///
/// A `settings` knob; it needs `config`'s `Xr.scene(true)`. Omitting it stops
/// the stream, and a new `url` or `hz` starts a new one. `url` is
/// `http://host[:port][/path]` and `hz` must be positive; the shell reads
/// depth back at most every 4th frame (30 a second at 120 Hz), so a higher
/// `hz` streams at that rate. A frame that arrives while the previous POST is
/// in flight replaces the waiting one, which counts as dropped; failures are
/// never retried. Frames go to loopback only, unless the operator allows
/// another origin (`--allow-sensor-upload ORIGIN` on desktop). Nothing
/// streams while the clock is paused, scrubbed or replaying, and nothing
/// reaches the model: `GET /state` reports the stream as `xr.stream`.
/// Desktop streams the stand-in room's depth; web does not stream.
let streamDepth : (streamOptions, t) => t
/// POST the left passthrough camera's image to `url`, `hz` frames a second,
/// one `functor.camera-frame/1` per request (see `docs/vr.md`, "Camera
/// streaming"); the options are last for piping.
///
/// A `settings` knob; it needs `config`'s `Xr.passthrough(true)`, and
/// streams only while the feed runs (`Xr.passthroughEnabled`). Otherwise it
/// behaves as `streamDepth`: a new `url` or `hz` starts a new stream, a frame
/// waiting behind a POST in flight is replaced and counted as dropped,
/// loopback only unless the operator allows another origin, and nothing
/// streams while the clock is paused, scrubbed or replaying. `GET /state`
/// reports it as `passthrough.camera`. Desktop streams the stand-in room as a
/// known pinhole camera at the view; web does not stream.
let streamCamera : (streamOptions, t) => t
